//? Title: Normalisation Layers: Axes, Invariances and Effective Learning Rates
//? Subtitle: Layer norm, RMSNorm, group and instance norm as one operation over different axes; their exact Jacobians and the gradient orthogonality they imply; norm growth and weight-decay equilibria of scale-invariant weights; signal propagation in depth; and seven normalisers on a 16-layer MLP, using datamunge's nn::layer_norm, nn::rms_norm, nn::group_norm, nn::instance_norm, nn::batch_norm, nn::normalize, nn::layer_norm_jacobian, nn::rms_norm_jacobian, nn::scale_norm, nn::dynamic_tanh and nn::weight_standardize
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Layer norm, RMSNorm, group norm, instance norm and batch norm all standardise activations. They differ only in the axes the mean and variance are taken over, and in whether they centre. This report checks each against a hand loop to \(10^{-15}\), shows that only batch norm couples the examples of a batch,
and measures the invariances to shifts and scalings, including where the \(\epsilon\) in the denominator breaks them. It derives the Jacobians in closed form and matches them to automatic differentiation. Layer norm's Jacobian has one exactly zero singular value and one equal to \(\epsilon/\sigma^3\).
As a result the gradient entering a normaliser sums to zero, and a weight matrix feeding one receives gradients orthogonal to itself. Under plain SGD its squared norm then grows by exactly \(\eta^2\lVert g\rVert^2\) per step, which holds on a digits classifier to a relative \(1.7\times10^{-11}\). With weight decay the norm settles near
a predicted equilibrium, or keeps collapsing and jumping back up with a spike in the loss. Through 40 random ReLU layers the activations follow \(c^l\) without normalisation and stay near \(\sqrt{1/2}\) with it. On a 16-layer MLP for digits, normalisation hardly matters at batch size 64. At batch size 4, batch norm stops learning
altogether, and Dynamic Tanh fails at both sizes because the signal vanishes before training starts.
)

\toc

> The problem

A deep network trains well only if the scale of its activations stays controlled from layer to layer. Normalisation layers do this directly: they subtract a mean and divide by a standard deviation computed from the activations themselves, then apply a learned gain \(\gamma\) and bias \(\beta\). Batch norm (Ioffe and Szegedy, 2015) takes the
statistics over the batch. Layer norm (Ba, Kiros and Hinton, 2016) takes them over the features of each example, instance norm (Ulyanov, Vedaldi and Lempitsky, 2016) over the positions of each channel, and group norm (Wu and He, 2018) over the positions and a group of channels. RMSNorm (Zhang and Sennrich, 2019) drops the centring.
Three questions are worth answering exactly. What do these layers compute? What do they do to gradients? And when do they help?

This report replaces the original example `examples/nn/0_foundations/0.2_reusable_layer_library/normalization_layers.cpp`. That example checked layer norm, RMSNorm and group norm against hand computations, showed batch independence, checked the group-norm limits and tabulated the invariances. All of that is kept. Added here are the Jacobians and their
consequences for training, signal propagation and a comparison on real data. The new functions are in `datamunge/nn/normalization_variants.hpp`.

> The method

>> One operation over different axes

For a tensor \(x\) and a set of axes \(A\), let \(\mu_A\) and \(\sigma^2_A\) be the mean and biased variance over \(A\), separately for every index of the other axes. The normalisers compute

$$
\hat x=\frac{x-\mu_A}{\sqrt{\sigma^2_A+\epsilon}},\qquad y=\gamma\odot\hat x+\beta,
$$
\alttext(x hat equals x minus mu A, over the square root of sigma squared A plus epsilon; y equals gamma times x hat plus beta.)

with \(A\) equal to the feature axis for layer norm, the spatial axes for instance norm, the spatial axes plus a group of \(C/G\) channels for group norm, and the batch axis (plus the spatial axes) for batch norm. RMSNorm divides by \(\sqrt{\overline{x^2}+\epsilon}\) without subtracting the mean. Group norm with \(G=1\) is layer norm over the whole image, and with \(G=C\) it is instance norm.
`nn::normalize(x, axes, center, eps)` performs this operation without parameters. Only batch norm puts the batch axis in \(A\), so it is the only one whose output for one example depends on the others.

Two cheaper relatives are included. ScaleNorm (Nguyen and Salazar, 2019) uses one scalar gain, \(y=g\,x/\sqrt{\lVert x\rVert^2+\epsilon}\), and equals RMSNorm when \(g=\sqrt d\). Dynamic Tanh (Zhu et al., 2025) drops the statistics altogether and computes \(y=\gamma\odot\tanh(\alpha x)+\beta\) with a learned scalar \(\alpha\). It was proposed as a replacement for layer norm in Transformers. Weight standardisation (Qiao et al., 2019)
normalises the weights instead of the activations: each output unit's incoming weights get mean 0 and variance 1.

>> Invariances

Ignoring \(\epsilon\), \(\mathrm{LN}(ax+c)=\operatorname{sign}(a)\,\mathrm{LN}(x)\) for any shift \(c\) and any \(a\ne0\), and \(\mathrm{RMS}(ax)=\operatorname{sign}(a)\,\mathrm{RMS}(x)\). With \(\epsilon\), scaling replaces \(\sigma^2\) by \(a^2\sigma^2\) but leaves \(\epsilon\) alone, so invariance holds only while \(a^2\sigma^2\gg\epsilon\). Inputs with variance near \(\epsilon\) are shrunk towards zero rather than blown up.

>> Jacobians and gradient orthogonality

For a vector \(x\in\mathbb R^d\) with \(\sigma=\sqrt{\mathrm{var}(x)+\epsilon}\) and \(\hat y=(x-\bar x)/\sigma\), differentiate \(\hat y_i=(x_i-\bar x)/\sigma\) using \(\partial\bar x/\partial x_j=1/d\) and \(\partial\sigma/\partial x_j=(x_j-\bar x)/(d\sigma)\):

$$
J_{\mathrm{LN}}=\frac1\sigma\Big(I-\frac1d\mathbf 1\mathbf 1^\top-\frac1d\hat y\hat y^\top\Big),\qquad J_{\mathrm{RMS}}=\frac1r\Big(I-\frac1d\hat y\hat y^\top\Big),
$$
\alttext(J LN equals one over sigma times, I minus one over d times the all-ones outer product, minus one over d times y hat y hat transpose. J RMS equals one over r times, I minus one over d times y hat y hat transpose.)

where for RMSNorm \(r=\sqrt{\overline{x^2}+\epsilon}\) and \(\hat y=x/r\) (`nn::layer_norm_jacobian`, `nn::rms_norm_jacobian`). Both are symmetric. \(J_{\mathrm{LN}}\mathbf 1=0\) exactly. Since \(\lVert\hat y\rVert^2=d\,\mathrm{var}/(\mathrm{var}+\epsilon)\), the centred input is an eigenvector with eigenvalue \(\epsilon/\sigma^3\), and the remaining \(d-2\) eigenvalues equal \(1/\sigma\). Likewise \(J_{\mathrm{RMS}}\) has eigenvalue \(\epsilon/r^3\) along \(x\) and \(1/r\) elsewhere.
Because \(J\) is symmetric, the gradient passed back, \(J\,\partial L/\partial y\), lies in the range of \(J\). It sums to zero over the features and is orthogonal to the centred input up to order \(\epsilon\).

The same structure acts on weights. If a weight matrix \(W\) feeds a normaliser with \(\epsilon=0\), then \(L(cW)=L(W)\) for every \(c>0\). Differentiating in \(c\) at \(c=1\) gives \(\langle\nabla L(W),W\rangle=0\), and differentiating \(L(cW)=L(W)\) in \(W\) gives \(\nabla L(cW)=\nabla L(W)/c\).

>> Norm growth and effective learning rate

A plain SGD step \(W'=W-\eta g\) with \(g\perp W\) gives, by Pythagoras,

$$
\lVert W'\rVert^2=\lVert W\rVert^2+\eta^2\lVert g\rVert^2,\qquad\text{so}\qquad \lVert W_t\rVert^2=\lVert W_0\rVert^2+\eta^2\sum_{s<t}\lVert g_s\rVert^2 .
$$
\alttext(The squared norm of W prime equals the squared norm of W plus eta squared times the squared norm of g, so the squared norm of W t equals the squared norm of W 0 plus eta squared times the sum over s less than t of the squared norm of g s.)

The norm can only grow. Since only the direction \(W/\lVert W\rVert\) matters and the gradient scales as \(1/\lVert W\rVert\), the step taken by the direction is \(\eta/\lVert W\rVert^2\) times the gradient at unit norm. Growth therefore lowers the effective learning rate automatically (van Laarhoven, 2017; Arora, Li and Lyu, 2019). Weight decay, \(W'=(1-\eta\lambda)W-\eta g\), pulls the other way. Writing \(\tilde g=g\lVert W\rVert\) for the gradient at unit norm, a fixed point of \(\lVert W\rVert^2\) on average satisfies

$$
\lVert W\rVert^4\big(1-(1-\eta\lambda)^2\big)=\eta^2\langle\lVert\tilde g\rVert^2\rangle\quad\Longrightarrow\quad \lVert W\rVert^2=\sqrt{\frac{\eta\,\langle\lVert\tilde g\rVert^2\rangle}{\lambda(2-\eta\lambda)}} .
$$
\alttext(The fourth power of the norm of W times, 1 minus the square of 1 minus eta lambda, equals eta squared times the mean squared norm of g tilde; therefore the squared norm of W equals the square root of eta times the mean squared norm of g tilde, over lambda times 2 minus eta lambda.)

>> Signal propagation

Take ReLU layers \(h_l=\mathrm{relu}(h_{l-1}W_l)\) of width \(n\) with weights \(\mathcal N(0,2c^2/n)\), which is He initialisation (He et al., 2015) times a gain \(c\). Given \(h_{l-1}\), each pre-activation is centred Gaussian with variance \(2c^2\,\overline{h_{l-1}^2}\), and ReLU keeps half of the second moment of a symmetric variable. So \(\mathbb E\,\overline{h_l^2}=c^2\,\overline{h_{l-1}^2}\) and the RMS changes by a factor \(c\) per layer. Over 40 layers, \(c=1.1\) gives \(45\) and \(c=0.9\) gives \(0.015\). With layer norm before each ReLU, every row of pre-activations has mean 0 and variance 1, and the RMS after ReLU is close to \(\sqrt{1/2}\) whatever \(c\) is.
Dynamic Tanh does not have this property. For small inputs \(\tanh(\alpha x)\approx\alpha x\), so at initialisation with \(\alpha=0.5\) each layer scales the RMS by about \(\alpha\).

> Simple examples

>> Hand checks, batch independence, groups and invariances

The program normalises \(x\in\mathbb R^{3\times6}\) (entries \(\mathcal N(2,3^2)\)) with layer norm and RMSNorm and compares the results with a loop over rows. It replaces one row and watches another, applies group norm with \(G=1,2,4,8\) to images \([2,4,4,8]\) whose channel \(c\) has offset \(3c\) and scale \(1+c\), and applies \(x\mapsto ax+c\) to a \([4,16]\) input for each normaliser (\(\epsilon=10^{-5}\)).

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/normalization_groups.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Tensor;
using dm::graphics::RGB;
const auto F64 = nn::DType::Float64;

double max_diff(const Tensor& a, const Tensor& b) { return nn::max(nn::abs(a - b)).item(); }

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const double eps = 1e-5;
  // 1. Layer norm and RMSNorm of x [3, 6] against a hand loop over each row.
  nn::ParameterStore p(1, F64);
  nn::Generator gen(1);
  const Tensor x = nn::normal({3, 6}, 2.0, 3.0, gen, F64);
  const Tensor y_ln = nn::layer_norm(p, "ln", x, eps), y_rms = nn::rms_norm(p, "rms", x, eps);
  double e_ln = 0, e_rms = 0;
  for (std::int64_t i = 0; i < 3; ++i) {
    double m = 0, ms = 0, v = 0;
    for (std::int64_t j = 0; j < 6; ++j) m += x.at({i, j}) / 6, ms += x.at({i, j}) * x.at({i, j}) / 6;
    for (std::int64_t j = 0; j < 6; ++j) v += (x.at({i, j}) - m) * (x.at({i, j}) - m) / 6;
    for (std::int64_t j = 0; j < 6; ++j) {
      e_ln = std::max(e_ln, std::abs(y_ln.at({i, j}) - (x.at({i, j}) - m) / std::sqrt(v + eps)));
      e_rms = std::max(e_rms, std::abs(y_rms.at({i, j}) - x.at({i, j}) / std::sqrt(ms + eps)));
    }
  }
  std::print("x [3, 6]: max |layer_norm − hand| = {:.1e}, max |rms_norm − hand| = {:.1e}; normalize(x, {{-1}}) vs layer_norm: {:.1e}.\n\n", e_ln, e_rms,
             max_diff(nn::normalize(x, {-1}), y_ln));
  std::print("| row 0 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const auto& [label, t] : std::vector<std::pair<std::string, Tensor>>{{"x", x}, {"layer norm", y_ln}, {"RMSNorm", y_rms}}) {
    std::print("| {} |", label);
    for (std::int64_t j = 0; j < 6; ++j) std::print(" {:.3f} |", t.at({0, j}));
    std::print("\n");
  }

  // 2. Batch independence: replace row 1 and compare row 0, for layer norm and for batch norm in training mode.
  std::vector<double> xv = x.to_vector<double>();
  for (std::int64_t j = 0; j < 6; ++j) xv[6 + j] = 100.0 * (j - 2);
  const Tensor xw = nn::from_vector(xv, {3, 6});
  nn::ParameterStore bp(1, F64), bs(2, F64);
  const Tensor b0 = nn::batch_norm(bp, bs, "bn", x, true), b1 = nn::batch_norm(bp, bs, "bn", xw, true);
  const Tensor l1 = nn::layer_norm(p, "ln", xw, eps);
  std::print("\nReplacing row 1 changes row 0's output by {:.1e} under layer norm and by {:.3f} under batch norm (training mode).\n",
             max_diff(nn::slice(y_ln, {0, 0}, {1, 6}), nn::slice(l1, {0, 0}, {1, 6})), max_diff(nn::slice(b0, {0, 0}, {1, 6}), nn::slice(b1, {0, 0}, {1, 6})));
  const Tensor xc = x - nn::mean(x, {1}, true);
  std::print("On rows with mean zero, RMSNorm and layer norm agree to {:.1e}.\n", max_diff(nn::layer_norm(p, "ln", xc, eps), nn::rms_norm(p, "rms", xc, eps)));

  // 3. Group norm of an image batch [2, 4, 4, 8] whose channel c has offset 3c and scale 1 + c.
  nn::Generator g2(2);
  const Tensor z = nn::normal({2, 4, 4, 8}, 0.0, 1.0, g2, F64);
  const Tensor cidx = nn::arange(0, 8, 1, F64);
  const Tensor img = z * (cidx + 1.0) + cidx * 3.0;
  std::print("\n| groups G | channels per group | statistics per example | max abs. difference from a hand computation |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  const std::vector<std::int64_t> groups{1, 2, 4, 8};
  std::vector<Tensor> out;
  for (std::int64_t G : groups) {
    out.push_back(nn::group_norm(p, std::format("gn{}", G), img, G, eps));
    // Hand: loop over (example, group) and standardise its H × W × C/G entries.
    const std::int64_t per = 8 / G;
    double err = 0;
    for (std::int64_t n = 0; n < 2; ++n)
      for (std::int64_t g = 0; g < G; ++g) {
        double m = 0, v = 0, cnt = 16.0 * per;
        for (std::int64_t h = 0; h < 4; ++h)
          for (std::int64_t w = 0; w < 4; ++w)
            for (std::int64_t c = g * per; c < (g + 1) * per; ++c) m += img.at({n, h, w, c}) / cnt;
        for (std::int64_t h = 0; h < 4; ++h)
          for (std::int64_t w = 0; w < 4; ++w)
            for (std::int64_t c = g * per; c < (g + 1) * per; ++c) v += std::pow(img.at({n, h, w, c}) - m, 2) / cnt;
        for (std::int64_t h = 0; h < 4; ++h)
          for (std::int64_t w = 0; w < 4; ++w)
            for (std::int64_t c = g * per; c < (g + 1) * per; ++c)
              err = std::max(err, std::abs(out.back().at({n, h, w, c}) - (img.at({n, h, w, c}) - m) / std::sqrt(v + eps)));
      }
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {:.1e} |\n", G, per, G, err);
  }
  std::print("\ninstance_norm vs group_norm with G = 8: {:.1e}; group_norm with G = 1 vs normalize over (H, W, C): {:.1e}.\n",
             max_diff(nn::instance_norm(p, "in", img, eps), out[3]), max_diff(nn::normalize(img, {1, 2, 3}), out[0]));
  // Per-channel mean and standard deviation of example 0, before and after.
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> cm(5), cs(5);
  for (std::size_t k = 0; k < 5; ++k) {
    const Tensor t = k == 0 ? img : out[k - 1];
    for (std::int64_t c = 0; c < 8; ++c) {
      double m = 0, v = 0;
      for (std::int64_t h = 0; h < 4; ++h)
        for (std::int64_t w = 0; w < 4; ++w) m += t.at({0, h, w, c}) / 16;
      for (std::int64_t h = 0; h < 4; ++h)
        for (std::int64_t w = 0; w < 4; ++w) v += std::pow(t.at({0, h, w, c}) - m, 2) / 16;
      cm[k].push_back(m), cs[k].push_back(std::sqrt(v));
    }
  }
  std::print("Example 0, per-channel mean after G = 1: ");
  for (double v : cm[1]) std::print("{:.2f} ", v);
  std::print("\nExample 0, per-channel mean after G = 8: ");
  for (double v : cm[4]) std::print("{:.1e} ", v);
  std::print("\nExample 0, per-channel std after G = 1: ");
  for (double v : cs[1]) std::print("{:.2f} ", v);
  std::print("\nExample 0, per-channel std after G = 2: ");
  for (double v : cs[2]) std::print("{:.2f} ", v);
  std::print("\n");

  // 4. Invariances of each normaliser to x -> a x + c on a [4, 16] input.
  nn::Generator g3(3);
  const Tensor u = nn::normal({4, 16}, 0.5, 2.0, g3, F64);
  nn::ParameterStore q(1, F64);
  const std::vector<std::pair<std::string, std::function<Tensor(const Tensor&)>>> norms{
      {"layer norm", [&](const Tensor& t) { return nn::layer_norm(q, "ln", t, eps); }},
      {"RMSNorm", [&](const Tensor& t) { return nn::rms_norm(q, "rms", t, eps); }},
      {"ScaleNorm", [&](const Tensor& t) { return nn::scale_norm(q, "sn", t, eps); }},
      {"Dynamic Tanh", [&](const Tensor& t) { return nn::dynamic_tanh(q, "dyt", t); }}};
  const std::vector<std::tuple<std::string, double, double>> maps{{"x + 5", 1, 5}, {"3x", 3, 0}, {"−2x", -2, 0}, {"3x + 5", 3, 5}, {"0.001x", 0.001, 0}};
  std::print("\n| max abs. change of the output under | layer norm | RMSNorm | ScaleNorm | Dynamic Tanh |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const auto& [label, a, c] : maps) {
    std::print("| x → {} |", label);
    for (const auto& [name, f] : norms) std::print(" {:.1e} |", max_diff(f(u * a + c), f(u)));
    std::print("\n");
  }

  // Figure: per-channel mean and standard deviation of example 0 before and after group norm.
  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  const std::vector<std::string> labels{"input", "G = 1 (layer norm)", "G = 2", "G = 4", "G = 8 (instance norm)"};
  const std::vector<RGB> cols{{148, 163, 184}, {37, 99, 235}, {22, 163, 74}, {234, 88, 12}, {30, 30, 30}};
  std::vector<double> ch(8);
  std::iota(ch.begin(), ch.end(), 0.0);
  for (int panel = 0; panel < 2; ++panel) {
    auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    const auto& data = panel == 0 ? cm : cs;
    for (std::size_t k = 1; k < 5; ++k) pl.lines(ch, data[k], labels[k], cols[k], 2.0).points(ch, data[k], "", cols[k], 4.5);
    pl.x_limits(-0.5, 7.5).x_label("channel c").title(panel == 0 ? "Mean of each channel after group norm" : "Std of each channel after group norm");
    if (panel == 0) pl.y_limits(-1.5, 2.6).y_label("mean over H × W, example 0");
    else pl.y_limits(0, 2.6).y_label("std over H × W, example 0");
    fig.add(pl);
  }
  fig.size(1100, 460).fit_panels();
  fig.save("build/normalization_groups.svg");
}
```
