\image(figures/normalization_groups.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Per-channel mean (left) and standard deviation (right) over the 4 × 4 positions of example 0 after group norm, for G = 1, 2, 4 and 8 groups of channels.)
\alttext(Left: the black G = 8 line is flat at 0; G = 1 rises steadily from about minus 1.2 to 1.4 across the channels; G = 2 and G = 4 zigzag around 0. Right: the black G = 8 line is flat at 1; G = 1 rises from 0.07 to 1.05; G = 2 and G = 4 go up and down between about 0.1 and 1.1.)

All four group sizes, and layer norm and RMSNorm, match the loops to rounding (at most \(1.1\times10^{-15}\)). Changing row 1 leaves row 0's layer norm output exactly unchanged, while batch norm in training mode moves it by 2.058. On rows with mean zero, RMSNorm and layer norm agree to \(10^{-16}\), since the only difference is the centring.

The figure shows what the grouping does. Instance norm (\(G=8\)) gives every channel mean 0 and standard deviation 1, which removes the deliberate differences between channels. Layer norm over the whole image (\(G=1\)) standardises all channels together, so their relative offsets and scales survive: means rise from −1.16 to 1.41 and standard deviations from 0.07 to 1.05. With \(G=2\) and \(G=4\), each group is standardised separately, so the channels zigzag around 0 and 1.

The invariance table confirms the algebra. Shifting by 5 changes layer norm by \(9\times10^{-16}\) but RMSNorm, ScaleNorm and Dynamic Tanh by 1.7 to 2.4. Scaling by 3 changes every normaliser except Dynamic Tanh by only \(2.5\times10^{-7}\) to \(4\times10^{-6}\). The residue is the \(\epsilon\) effect: \(\epsilon\) is not scaled with the input. Scaling by \(-2\) flips the sign of the outputs. Scaling by 0.001 makes the variance \(4\times10^{-6}\), below \(\epsilon\), and the outputs change by 1.1.
ScaleNorm is less affected (0.23) because its \(\epsilon\) is added to \(\lVert x\rVert^2\), which is \(d=16\) times the mean square. Dynamic Tanh is elementwise and invariant to nothing.

> Checking the theory

>> Jacobians, orthogonality, depth and norm growth

The program compares the closed-form Jacobians with automatic differentiation at a random \(x\in\mathbb R^{16}\) and computes their singular values. It checks the gradient identities on a batch of 32 rows and on a weight \(W\in\mathbb R^{10\times8}\) feeding layer norm with \(\epsilon=0\). It propagates 64 random inputs through 40 ReLU layers of width 256, with and without layer norm. Finally, it trains \(\mathrm{logits}=\mathrm{relu}(\mathrm{LN}(XW_1))W_2+b\) on all 1797 digits by hand-written SGD (\(\eta=0.2\), batches of 64, 12000 steps). Because of the layer norm, \(W_1\) is scale-invariant. It is trained without weight decay and with \(\lambda=10^{-3}\) and \(10^{-2}\) on \(W_1\).

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/normalization_theory.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Tensor;
using dm::graphics::RGB;
const auto F64 = nn::DType::Float64;

double max_diff(const Tensor& a, const Tensor& b) { return nn::max(nn::abs(a - b)).item(); }
double fdot(const Tensor& a, const Tensor& b) { return nn::sum(a * b).item(); }
double fnorm(const Tensor& a) { return std::sqrt(fdot(a, a)); }

// A leaf copy of t that requires a gradient.
Tensor leaf(const Tensor& t) {
  Tensor l = t.detach().clone();
  l.requires_grad_();
  return l;
}

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  // 1. Exact Jacobians against autodiff, and their singular values (d = 16).
  nn::Generator g(4);
  const std::int64_t d = 16;
  const Tensor x = nn::normal({d}, 0.7, 1.3, g, F64);
  const double eps = 1e-5;
  const Tensor j_ln = nn::layer_norm_jacobian(x, eps), j_rms = nn::rms_norm_jacobian(x, eps);
  const Tensor a_ln = nn::jacobian([&](const Tensor& v) { return nn::normalize(v, {0}, true, eps); }, x);
  const Tensor a_rms = nn::jacobian([&](const Tensor& v) { return nn::normalize(v, {0}, false, eps); }, x);
  std::print("Jacobians at x ∈ R^16: max |closed form − autodiff| = {:.1e} (layer norm), {:.1e} (RMSNorm).\n", max_diff(j_ln, a_ln), max_diff(j_rms, a_rms));
  const double mu = nn::mean(x).item(), var = nn::mean(nn::square(x - mu)).item(), ms = nn::mean(nn::square(x)).item();
  const double sigma = std::sqrt(var + eps), r = std::sqrt(ms + eps);
  const Tensor s_ln = nn::svd(j_ln, false).s, s_rms = nn::svd(j_rms, false).s;
  std::print("Layer norm: σ = {:.4f}; singular values: {:.6f} (×{}) then {:.3e} and {:.1e}; predicted 1/σ = {:.6f} and ε/σ³ = {:.3e}.\n", sigma, s_ln.at({0}), d - 2,
             s_ln.at({d - 2}), s_ln.at({d - 1}), 1 / sigma, eps / (sigma * sigma * sigma));
  std::print("RMSNorm: r = {:.4f}; singular values: {:.6f} (×{}) then {:.3e}; predicted 1/r = {:.6f} and ε/r³ = {:.3e}.\n", r, s_rms.at({0}), d - 1, s_rms.at({d - 1}), 1 / r,
             eps / (r * r * r));
  double spread_ln = 0, spread_rms = 0;
  for (std::int64_t k = 0; k < d - 2; ++k) spread_ln = std::max(spread_ln, std::abs(s_ln.at({k}) - 1 / sigma));
  for (std::int64_t k = 0; k < d - 1; ++k) spread_rms = std::max(spread_rms, std::abs(s_rms.at({k}) - 1 / r));
  std::print("Largest deviation of the repeated singular values from 1/σ, 1/r: {:.1e}, {:.1e}.\n", spread_ln, spread_rms);

  // 2. Gradients through a normaliser: orthogonal to 1 and to the centred input; scale-invariant weights.
  const Tensor X = nn::normal({32, 10}, 0.3, 1.0, g, F64), R = nn::normal({32, 8}, 0.0, 1.0, g, F64);
  Tensor H = leaf(nn::normal({32, 8}, 1.0, 2.0, g, F64));
  const Tensor gh = nn::grad(nn::sum(nn::normalize(H, {-1}) * R), {H})[0];
  const Tensor hc = H - nn::mean(H, {1}, true);
  std::print("\nUpstream gradient of L = Σ r ⊙ LN(h) for 32 rows: max |Σ_j ∂L/∂h_j| = {:.1e}; max cosine with the centred row = {:.1e}.\n",
             nn::max(nn::abs(nn::sum(gh, {1}))).item(), nn::max(nn::abs(nn::sum(gh * hc, {1}) / nn::sqrt(nn::sum(gh * gh, {1}) * nn::sum(hc * hc, {1})))).item());
  Tensor W = leaf(nn::normal({10, 8}, 0.0, 0.5, g, F64));
  auto loss_w = [&](const Tensor& w) { return nn::sum(nn::normalize(nn::matmul(X, w), {-1}, true, 0.0) * R); };
  const Tensor gw = nn::grad(loss_w(W), {W})[0];
  Tensor W3 = leaf(W * 3.0);
  const Tensor gw3 = nn::grad(loss_w(W3), {W3})[0];
  std::print("Weight W [10, 8] feeding layer norm (ε = 0): cos(∇W, W) = {:.1e}; L(3W) − L(W) = {:.1e}; max |∇L(3W) − ∇L(W)/3| = {:.1e}.\n", fdot(gw, W) / (fnorm(gw) * fnorm(W)),
             loss_w(W3).item() - loss_w(W).item(), max_diff(gw3, gw / 3.0));

  // 3. Signal propagation through 40 ReLU layers of width 256 with weights N(0, 2c²/n): mean square multiplies by c² per layer.
  const int L = 40, n = 256;
  const std::vector<double> gains{0.9, 1.0, 1.1};
  std::vector<double> layers(L + 1);
  std::iota(layers.begin(), layers.end(), 0.0);
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> rms_plain(gains.size()), rms_ln(gains.size());
  for (std::size_t k = 0; k < gains.size(); ++k) {
    nn::Generator gk(10 + k);
    Tensor h = nn::normal({64, n}, 0.0, 1.0, gk, F64), hn = h;
    rms_plain[k].push_back(std::sqrt(nn::mean(nn::square(h)).item()));
    rms_ln[k].push_back(rms_plain[k].back());
    for (int l = 1; l <= L; ++l) {
      const Tensor w = nn::normal({n, n}, 0.0, gains[k] * std::sqrt(2.0 / n), gk, F64);
      h = nn::relu(nn::matmul(h, w));
      hn = nn::relu(nn::normalize(nn::matmul(hn, w), {-1}));
      rms_plain[k].push_back(std::sqrt(nn::mean(nn::square(h)).item()));
      rms_ln[k].push_back(std::sqrt(nn::mean(nn::square(hn)).item()));
    }
    std::print("{}gain c = {:.1f}: RMS after 40 layers {:.3e} without normalisation (predicted c^40 × RMS₀ = {:.3e}), {:.4f} with layer norm before each ReLU.\n", k == 0 ? "\n" : "",
               gains[k], rms_plain[k].back(), std::pow(gains[k], L) * rms_plain[k][0], rms_ln[k].back());
  }
  std::print("With layer norm the RMS after every layer lies in [{:.4f}, {:.4f}] for all three gains; √(1/2) = {:.4f}.\n",
             [&] { double m = 9; for (auto& v : rms_ln) for (std::size_t l = 1; l < v.size(); ++l) m = std::min(m, v[l]); return m; }(),
             [&] { double m = 0; for (auto& v : rms_ln) for (std::size_t l = 1; l < v.size(); ++l) m = std::max(m, v[l]); return m; }(), std::sqrt(0.5));

  // 4. Effective learning rate: digits, logits = relu(LN(X W1)) W2 + b, plain SGD by hand. W1 is scale-invariant.
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits();
  const std::int64_t N = 1797, D = 64, Hd = 64;
  std::vector<double> v(static_cast<std::size_t>(N * D));
  for (std::int64_t j = 0; j < D; ++j) {
    const auto c = digits.get_column<double>(std::format("pixel_{}", j)).values();
    for (std::int64_t i = 0; i < N; ++i) v[i * D + j] = c[i] / 16.0;
  }
  const Tensor Xd = nn::from_vector(v, {N, D});
  const Tensor yd = nn::cast(nn::from_vector(digits.get_column<double>("digit").values(), {N}), nn::DType::Int64);
  const double lr = 0.2;
  const int steps = 12000, every = 120;
  std::vector<double> axis;
  struct Run { double wd; std::vector<double> norm2, pred2, eq2, loss; double cum_g2 = 0; };
  std::vector<Run> runs{{0.0}, {1e-3}, {1e-2}};
  for (auto& run : runs) {
    nn::Generator gi(7);
    Tensor W1 = leaf(nn::normal({D, Hd}, 0.0, std::sqrt(1.0 / D), gi, F64)), W2 = leaf(nn::normal({Hd, 10}, 0.0, std::sqrt(1.0 / Hd), gi, F64)),
           b = leaf(nn::zeros({10}, F64));
    const double n0 = fdot(W1, W1);
    std::mt19937_64 rng(1);
    std::uniform_int_distribution<std::int64_t> pick(0, N - 1);
    double mean_gt2 = 0;  // running average of ||g̃||² = ||g||² ||W1||² over the last quarter
    int count = 0;
    double win_loss = 0;  // largest minibatch loss in the current record window
    for (int s = 1; s <= steps; ++s) {
      std::vector<std::int64_t> idx(64);
      for (auto& i : idx) i = pick(rng);
      const Tensor ix = nn::from_vector(idx, {64});
      const Tensor logits = nn::matmul(nn::relu(nn::normalize(nn::matmul(nn::gather(Xd, ix, 0), W1), {-1}, true, 1e-12)), W2) + b;
      const Tensor ce = nn::sparse_softmax_cross_entropy(logits, nn::gather(yd, ix, 0));
      win_loss = std::max(win_loss, ce.item());
      const auto gr = nn::grad(ce, {W1, W2, b});
      const double g2 = fdot(gr[0], gr[0]), w2 = fdot(W1, W1);
      run.cum_g2 += g2;
      if (s > 3 * steps / 4) mean_gt2 += g2 * w2, ++count;
      {
        nn::NoGradGuard ng;
        W1 = leaf(W1 * (1.0 - lr * run.wd) - gr[0] * lr);
        W2 = leaf(W2 - gr[1] * lr);
        b = leaf(b - gr[2] * lr);
      }
      if (s % every == 0) {
        if (&run == &runs[0]) axis.push_back(s);
        run.norm2.push_back(fdot(W1, W1));
        run.pred2.push_back(n0 + lr * lr * run.cum_g2);
        run.loss.push_back(win_loss);
        win_loss = 0;
      }
    }
    mean_gt2 /= count;
    const double eq = run.wd > 0 ? std::sqrt(lr * mean_gt2 / (run.wd * (2.0 - lr * run.wd))) : 0.0;
    run.eq2 = {eq};
    // Test-style accuracy on all images (the point here is the norm, not generalisation).
    const Tensor logits = nn::matmul(nn::relu(nn::normalize(nn::matmul(Xd, W1), {-1}, true, 1e-12)), W2) + b;
    const double acc = nn::mean(nn::cast(nn::equal(nn::argmax(logits, 1), yd), F64)).item();
    if (run.wd == 0)
      std::print("\nSGD (lr {}) without weight decay: ||W1||² grows from {:.3f} to {:.3f}; predicted ||W1₀||² + η² Σ||g||² = {:.3f} (relative difference {:.1e}); accuracy on all images {:.3f}.\n",
                 lr, n0, run.norm2.back(), run.pred2.back(), std::abs(run.norm2.back() / run.pred2.back() - 1), acc);
    else
      std::print("SGD with weight decay λ = {}: ||W1||² over the last quarter averages {:.3f} (last {:.3f}); equilibrium √(η⟨||g̃||²⟩ / (λ(2 − ηλ))) = {:.3f}; accuracy {:.3f}.\n", run.wd,
                 [&] { double m = 0; std::size_t c0 = run.norm2.size() * 3 / 4; for (std::size_t i = c0; i < run.norm2.size(); ++i) m += run.norm2[i]; return m / (run.norm2.size() - c0); }(),
                 run.norm2.back(), eq, acc);
  }
  double maxrel = 0;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < runs[0].norm2.size(); ++i) maxrel = std::max(maxrel, std::abs(runs[0].norm2[i] / runs[0].pred2[i] - 1));
  std::print("Without weight decay the identity holds at every recorded step to a relative {:.1e}.\n", maxrel);
  for (std::size_t k = 1; k < runs.size(); ++k) {
    const auto& rr = runs[k];
    std::vector<double> calm;
    for (std::size_t i = 1; i < rr.norm2.size(); ++i) if (rr.norm2[i] <= 1.2 * rr.norm2[i - 1]) calm.push_back(rr.loss[i]);
    std::ranges::sort(calm);
    std::print("λ = {}: median over windows without a jump of the largest minibatch loss {:.4f}; ", rr.wd, calm[calm.size() / 2]);
    int jumps = 0;
    for (std::size_t i = 1; i < rr.norm2.size(); ++i)
      if (rr.norm2[i] > 1.2 * rr.norm2[i - 1]) {
        if (jumps++ < 6) std::print("{}step {}: ||W1||² {:.2f} → {:.2f}, largest minibatch loss {:.3f}", jumps > 1 ? "; " : "jumps of > 20%: ", axis[i], rr.norm2[i - 1], rr.norm2[i], rr.loss[i]);
      }
    std::print(" ({} jumps in all).\n", jumps);
  }

  // Figure.
  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(2, 2);
  const RGB blue{37, 99, 235}, orange{234, 88, 12}, green{22, 163, 74}, grey{148, 163, 184}, black{30, 30, 30};
  const std::vector<RGB> gcols{blue, black, orange};
  {
    auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    std::vector<double> idx(d), l_ln, l_rms;
    std::iota(idx.begin(), idx.end(), 1.0);
    for (std::int64_t k = 0; k < d; ++k) l_ln.push_back(std::log10(s_ln.at({k}))), l_rms.push_back(std::log10(std::max(s_rms.at({k}), 1e-300)));
    pl.points(idx, l_rms, "RMSNorm", orange, 6).points(idx, l_ln, "layer norm", blue, 4.5);
    pl.x_limits(0.5, d + 0.5).y_limits(-22, 4).title("Singular values of the Jacobian (d = 16)").x_label("index").y_label("log10 singular value");
    fig.add(pl);
  }
  {
    auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    for (std::size_t k = 0; k < gains.size(); ++k) {
      std::vector<double> lp, pr, ll;
      for (int l = 0; l <= L; ++l) {
        lp.push_back(std::log10(rms_plain[k][l])), pr.push_back(std::log10(std::pow(gains[k], l) * rms_plain[k][0])), ll.push_back(std::log10(rms_ln[k][l]));
      }
      pl.lines(layers, pr, k == 0 ? "c^l (theory)" : "", grey, 1.2).lines(layers, lp, std::format("no norm, c = {:.1f}", gains[k]), gcols[k], 2.2);
      pl.lines(layers, ll, k == 0 ? "layer norm, every c" : "", green, 2.2);
    }
    pl.x_limits(0, L).y_limits(-2.5, 3).title("Signal through 40 ReLU layers").x_label("layer").y_label("log10 RMS of activations");
    fig.add(pl);
  }
  const std::vector<RGB> rcols{black, blue, orange};
  {
    auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    pl.lines(axis, runs[0].pred2, "||W1₀||² + η² Σ ||g||²", grey, 5).lines(axis, runs[0].norm2, "||W1||², no decay", black, 1.8);
    pl.x_limits(0, steps).y_limits(std::floor(runs[0].norm2.front()) - 2, std::ceil(runs[0].norm2.back()) + 4).title("Norm growth of a scale-invariant weight").x_label("SGD step").y_label("||W1||²");
    fig.add(pl);
  }
  {
    auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    double top = 0;
    for (std::size_t k = 1; k < runs.size(); ++k) {
      pl.lines(axis, runs[k].norm2, std::format("||W1||², λ = {}", runs[k].wd), rcols[k], 1.8).abline_h(runs[k].eq2[0], rcols[k], 1);
      top = std::max(top, *std::ranges::max_element(runs[k].norm2));
    }
    pl.x_limits(0, steps).y_limits(0, top * 1.4).title("With weight decay: equilibrium norm").x_label("SGD step").y_label("||W1||² (lines: predicted equilibrium)");
    fig.add(pl);
  }
  fig.size(1100, 1000).fit_panels();
  fig.save("build/normalization_theory.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Jacobians at x ∈ R^16: max |closed form − autodiff| = 1.1e-16 (layer norm), 1.1e-16 (RMSNorm).
Layer norm: σ = 1.6055; singular values: 0.622849 (×14) then 2.416e-06 and 1.6e-17; predicted 1/σ = 0.622849 and ε/σ³ = 2.416e-06.
RMSNorm: r = 1.7079; singular values: 0.585528 (×15) then 2.007e-06; predicted 1/r = 0.585528 and ε/r³ = 2.007e-06.
Largest deviation of the repeated singular values from 1/σ, 1/r: 2.2e-16, 3.3e-16.

Upstream gradient of L = Σ r ⊙ LN(h) for 32 rows: max |Σ_j ∂L/∂h_j| = 6.7e-16; max cosine with the centred row = 7.2e-06.
Weight W [10, 8] feeding layer norm (ε = 0): cos(∇W, W) = -2.7e-17; L(3W) − L(W) = 5.3e-15; max |∇L(3W) − ∇L(W)/3| = 1.1e-15.

gain c = 0.9: RMS after 40 layers 1.832e-02 without normalisation (predicted c^40 × RMS₀ = 1.497e-02), 0.7024 with layer norm before each ReLU.
gain c = 1.0: RMS after 40 layers 1.255e+00 without normalisation (predicted c^40 × RMS₀ = 9.956e-01), 0.6782 with layer norm before each ReLU.
gain c = 1.1: RMS after 40 layers 4.687e+01 without normalisation (predicted c^40 × RMS₀ = 4.493e+01), 0.7121 with layer norm before each ReLU.
With layer norm the RMS after every layer lies in [0.6752, 0.7457] for all three gains; √(1/2) = 0.7071.

SGD (lr 0.2) without weight decay: ||W1||² grows from 64.035 to 73.662; predicted ||W1₀||² + η² Σ||g||² = 73.662 (relative difference 1.7e-11); accuracy on all images 1.000.
SGD with weight decay λ = 0.001: ||W1||² over the last quarter averages 13.657 (last 15.567); equilibrium √(η⟨||g̃||²⟩ / (λ(2 − ηλ))) = 16.855; accuracy 1.000.
SGD with weight decay λ = 0.01: ||W1||² over the last quarter averages 18.031 (last 19.654); equilibrium √(η⟨||g̃||²⟩ / (λ(2 − ηλ))) = 17.769; accuracy 0.992.
Without weight decay the identity holds at every recorded step to a relative 1.7e-11.
λ = 0.001: median over windows without a jump of the largest minibatch loss 0.0065; jumps of > 20%: step 5880: ||W1||² 12.00 → 22.84, largest minibatch loss 7.972; step 10560: ||W1||² 5.12 → 19.96, largest minibatch loss 4.352 (2 jumps in all).
λ = 0.01: median over windows without a jump of the largest minibatch loss 1.7165; jumps of > 20%: step 2160: ||W1||² 12.17 → 14.87, largest minibatch loss 1.859; step 2400: ||W1||² 13.88 → 17.61, largest minibatch loss 1.678; step 2880: ||W1||² 12.86 → 16.69, largest minibatch loss 1.568; step 3120: ||W1||² 12.22 → 17.96, largest minibatch loss 2.131; step 3600: ||W1||² 16.15 → 21.41, largest minibatch loss 2.727; step 4680: ||W1||² 12.55 → 16.87, largest minibatch loss 4.210 (23 jumps in all).
\image(figures/normalization_theory.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Top left: singular values of the two Jacobians on a log scale. Top right: RMS of the activations through 40 random ReLU layers for gains 0.9, 1 and 1.1 without normalisation, the prediction c to the power l (grey), and with layer norm (green, all gains). Bottom left: the squared norm of the scale-invariant weight under plain SGD and the prediction from the summed squared gradients (wide grey line). Bottom right: the same with weight decay; horizontal lines mark the predicted equilibria.)
\alttext(Top left: 14 blue and 15 orange points at about minus 0.2, then a blue point near minus 5.6, an orange one near minus 5.7, and a last blue point near minus 16.8. Top right: the orange line rises steadily to about 1.7, the blue line falls to about minus 1.7, each close to its grey line; the black line wanders slightly above 0; the green lines sit flat at about minus 0.15. Bottom left: the black line rises from 64 to about 73.7 within 2000 steps and stays there, exactly on the wide grey line. Bottom right: the orange line drops quickly from 42 and then fluctuates between about 10 and 29, around its line near 17.8; the blue line decays from 64 to about 12 by step 5900, jumps to 23, decays to about 5 by step 10500, and jumps to 20; its line is near 16.9.)

The closed forms agree with automatic differentiation to \(1.1\times10^{-16}\). The singular values are as derived. Layer norm has 14 equal to \(1/\sigma=0.622849\), one equal to \(\epsilon/\sigma^3=2.416\times10^{-6}\) and one at rounding level (\(1.6\times10^{-17}\)). RMSNorm has 15 equal to \(1/r\) and one equal to \(\epsilon/r^3\). As a consequence, the gradient entering layer norm sums to \(7\times10^{-16}\) per row, and its cosine with the centred input is at most \(7.2\times10^{-6}\), the size of the \(\epsilon\) term. For the weight feeding layer norm with \(\epsilon=0\), the cosine between gradient and weight is \(-3\times10^{-17}\), \(L(3W)\) equals \(L(W)\) to \(5\times10^{-15}\), and \(\nabla L(3W)=\nabla L(W)/3\) to \(10^{-15}\).

Without normalisation the RMS follows \(c^l\). After 40 layers it reaches 46.9 for \(c=1.1\) (predicted 44.9), 0.018 for \(c=0.9\) (predicted 0.015) and 1.26 for \(c=1\) (predicted 1.00). The ratios of 1.04 to 1.26 are the drift of a single finite-width run, because the recursion holds for the expected mean square. With layer norm the RMS stays between 0.675 and 0.746 after every layer, close to \(\sqrt{1/2}=0.707\), for all three gains.

Without weight decay, the squared norm of \(W_1\) grows from 64.04 to 73.66 and matches \(\lVert W_0\rVert^2+\eta^2\sum\lVert g\rVert^2\) at every recorded step to a relative \(1.7\times10^{-11}\). Most of the growth happens in the first 2000 steps, while the gradients are large. With \(\lambda=10^{-2}\) the norm quickly drops and fluctuates around 18.0 over the last quarter of training, against the predicted equilibrium of 17.8. With \(\lambda=10^{-3}\) it never settles. It decays towards zero, because the training loss is almost zero and the gradients that would push it out are tiny, and the effective learning rate \(\eta/\lVert W\rVert^2\) keeps rising.
At steps 5880 and 10560 the training destabilises. The largest minibatch loss in those windows is 7.97 and 4.35, against a typical window's largest loss of 0.0065, and the norm jumps from 12.0 to 22.8 and from 5.1 to 20.0. This periodic collapse and recovery is the behaviour described by Lobacheva et al. (2021) for scale-invariant networks with weight decay, and the equilibrium formula does not apply to it.

> A dataset: seven normalisers on a deep MLP

The digits data (Alpaydin and Kaynak, 1998) has 1797 images of 8 × 8 pixels, scaled to [0, 1]. After a fixed shuffle, 1437 images train and 360 test. The network is 16 blocks of [dense 64 → normaliser → ReLU] followed by a dense layer with 10 outputs, with He-normal weights. The normaliser is none, batch norm (momentum 0.99, \(\epsilon=10^{-3}\)), layer norm, RMSNorm, ScaleNorm or Dynamic Tanh (\(\alpha_0=0.5\)).
Alternatively, each dense layer uses standardised weights scaled by \(\sqrt{2/n_{\mathrm{in}}}\). Adam (step \(10^{-3}\)) runs for 500 steps with batches of 64 or of 4, with two seeds, in single precision so that the 28 runs stay short. Training loss and test accuracy are measured in inference mode, which uses batch norm's moving averages.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/normalization_digits.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
namespace init = dm::nn::init;
using nn::Tensor;
using dm::graphics::RGB;
const auto F32 = nn::DType::Float32;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits();
  const std::int64_t n = 1797, d = 64;
  std::vector<double> v(static_cast<std::size_t>(n * d));
  for (std::int64_t j = 0; j < d; ++j) {
    const auto c = digits.get_column<double>(std::format("pixel_{}", j)).values();
    for (std::int64_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) v[i * d + j] = c[i] / 16.0;
  }
  const Tensor X = nn::cast(nn::from_vector(v, {n, d}), F32);
  const Tensor y = nn::cast(nn::from_vector(digits.get_column<double>("digit").values(), {n}), nn::DType::Int64);
  std::vector<std::int64_t> perm(n);
  std::iota(perm.begin(), perm.end(), 0);
  std::mt19937_64 shuffle_rng(0);
  std::shuffle(perm.begin(), perm.end(), shuffle_rng);
  const std::int64_t n_test = 360, n_train = n - n_test;
  const Tensor ptr = nn::from_vector(std::vector<std::int64_t>(perm.begin(), perm.begin() + n_train), {n_train});
  const Tensor pte = nn::from_vector(std::vector<std::int64_t>(perm.begin() + n_train, perm.end()), {n_test});
  const Tensor Xtr = nn::gather(X, ptr, 0), ytr = nn::gather(y, ptr, 0), Xte = nn::gather(X, pte, 0), yte = nn::gather(y, pte, 0);

  // A 16-layer MLP of width 64: [dense -> normaliser -> ReLU] x 16 -> dense(10).
  const int depth = 16, width = 64, steps = 500, every = 50;
  const std::vector<std::string> methods{"none", "batch norm", "layer norm", "RMSNorm", "ScaleNorm", "Dynamic Tanh", "weight standardisation"};
  const std::vector<RGB> cols{{148, 163, 184}, {234, 88, 12}, {37, 99, 235}, {22, 163, 74}, {147, 51, 234}, {219, 39, 119}, {30, 30, 30}};
  const std::vector<std::int64_t> batches{64, 4};
  std::vector<double> axis, init_rms(methods.size()), bn_batch_acc;
  for (int s = every; s <= steps; s += every) axis.push_back(s);
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> curve(methods.size(), std::vector<double>(axis.size(), 0.0));
  std::vector<std::vector<std::vector<double>>> test_acc(methods.size(), std::vector<std::vector<double>>(batches.size()));
  std::print("| normaliser | batch 64: train loss | batch 64: test accuracy % | batch 4: train loss | batch 4: test accuracy % |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t m = 0; m < methods.size(); ++m) {
    std::vector<std::string> cells;
    for (std::size_t bi = 0; bi < batches.size(); ++bi) {
      std::vector<double> losses, accs;
      for (std::uint64_t seed : {1, 2}) {
        nn::ParameterStore p(seed, F32), stats(seed + 100, F32);
        auto f = [&](const Tensor& x, bool training, bool hidden = false) {
          Tensor h = x;
          for (int l = 0; l < depth; ++l) {
            const std::string name = std::format("l{}", l);
            if (m == 6) {
              const std::int64_t in = h.size(1);
              const Tensor W = p.get(name + "/W", {in, width}, init::he_normal()), b = p.get(name + "/b", {width}, init::zeros());
              h = nn::matmul(h, nn::weight_standardize(W)) * std::sqrt(2.0 / static_cast<double>(in)) + b;
            } else {
              h = nn::dense(p, name, h, width, {.init = init::he_normal()});
            }
            switch (m) {
              case 1: h = nn::batch_norm(p, stats, name + "/bn", h, training); break;
              case 2: h = nn::layer_norm(p, name + "/ln", h); break;
              case 3: h = nn::rms_norm(p, name + "/rms", h); break;
              case 4: h = nn::scale_norm(p, name + "/sn", h); break;
              case 5: h = nn::dynamic_tanh(p, name + "/dyt", h); break;
              default: break;
            }
            h = nn::relu(h);
          }
          return hidden ? h : nn::dense(p, "out", h, 10);
        };
        (void)f(nn::slice(Xtr, {0, 0}, {4, d}), false);
        if (bi == 0 && seed == 1) {
          nn::NoGradGuard ng;
          init_rms[m] = std::sqrt(nn::mean(nn::square(f(nn::slice(Xtr, {0, 0}, {256, d}), true, true))).item());
        }
        nn::Optimizer opt(p, 1e-3, {.kind = nn::OptimizerKind::Adam});
        std::mt19937_64 rng(seed);
        std::uniform_int_distribution<std::int64_t> pick(0, n_train - 1);
        const std::int64_t B = batches[bi];
        for (int s = 1; s <= steps; ++s) {
          std::vector<std::int64_t> idx(static_cast<std::size_t>(B));
          for (auto& i : idx) i = pick(rng);
          const Tensor ix = nn::from_vector(idx, {B});
          opt.minimize(nn::sparse_softmax_cross_entropy(f(nn::gather(Xtr, ix, 0), true), nn::gather(ytr, ix, 0)));
          if (bi == 0 && s % every == 0) {
            nn::NoGradGuard ng;
            curve[m][s / every - 1] += nn::sparse_softmax_cross_entropy(f(Xtr, false), ytr).item() / 2;
          }
        }
        nn::NoGradGuard ng;
        losses.push_back(nn::sparse_softmax_cross_entropy(f(Xtr, false), ytr).item());
        accs.push_back(100 * nn::mean(nn::cast(nn::equal(nn::argmax(f(Xte, false), 1), yte), F32)).item());
        if (m == 1 && bi == 1) {
          // Batch norm normalising each test batch of 4 with its own statistics (training mode), as during training.
          double correct = 0;
          for (std::int64_t i = 0; i < n_test; i += 4) {
            const Tensor xb = nn::slice(Xte, {i, 0}, {4, d}), yb = nn::slice(yte, {i}, {4});
            correct += nn::sum(nn::cast(nn::equal(nn::argmax(f(xb, true), 1), yb), F32)).item();
          }
          bn_batch_acc.push_back(100 * correct / n_test);
        }
      }
      test_acc[m][bi] = accs;
      cells.push_back(std::format("{:.3f}, {:.3f}", losses[0], losses[1]));
      cells.push_back(std::format("{:.1f}, {:.1f}", accs[0], accs[1]));
    }
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", methods[m], cells[0], cells[1], cells[2], cells[3]);
  }
  for (std::size_t m = 0; m < methods.size(); ++m) {
    int first = -1;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < axis.size(); ++i)
      if (curve[m][i] < 0.1) { first = static_cast<int>(axis[i]); break; }
    std::print("{}: training loss (batch 64, mean of 2 seeds) {:.3f} at step {}, {:.4f} at step {}; first below 0.1 at step {}.\n", methods[m], curve[m][0], every, curve[m].back(), steps,
               first < 0 ? std::string("never") : std::to_string(first));
  }

  std::print("\nBatch norm trained with batches of 4, test accuracy when each test batch of 4 is normalised with its own statistics: {:.1f}%, {:.1f}%.\n", bn_batch_acc[0], bn_batch_acc[1]);
  std::print("RMS of the last hidden layer at initialisation (seed 1, 256 training images):");
  for (std::size_t m = 0; m < methods.size(); ++m) std::print("{} {} {:.2e}", m ? "," : "", methods[m], init_rms[m]);
  std::print(".\n");
  const std::vector<std::string> short_names{"none", "batch norm", "layer norm", "RMSNorm", "ScaleNorm", "Dynamic Tanh", "weight std."};
  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  {
    auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    for (std::size_t m = 0; m < methods.size(); ++m) {
      std::vector<double> lc;
      for (double c : curve[m]) lc.push_back(std::log10(std::max(c, 1e-6)));
      pl.lines(axis, lc, short_names[m], cols[m], 2.2);
    }
    pl.x_limits(0, steps).y_limits(-6, 7).title("Training loss, batch 64").x_label("Adam step").y_label("log10 cross-entropy (train set)");
    fig.add(pl);
  }
  {
    auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    for (std::size_t m = 0; m < methods.size(); ++m)
      for (std::size_t bi = 0; bi < batches.size(); ++bi) {
        std::vector<double> xs, ys;
        for (double a : test_acc[m][bi]) xs.push_back(m + (bi == 0 ? -0.15 : 0.15)), ys.push_back(a);
        pl.points(xs, ys, m == 0 ? (bi == 0 ? "batch 64" : "batch 4") : "", bi == 0 ? RGB{37, 99, 235} : RGB{234, 88, 12}, 6);
      }
    pl.x_limits(-0.5, methods.size() - 0.5).x_tick_labels({"none", "BN", "LN", "RMS", "Scale", "DyT", "WS"}).title("Test accuracy, each seed").x_label("normaliser")
        .y_label("test accuracy (%)").y_limits(0, 118);
    fig.add(pl);
  }
  fig.size(1100, 460).fit_panels();
  fig.save("build/normalization_digits.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| normaliser | batch 64: train loss | batch 64: test accuracy % | batch 4: train loss | batch 4: test accuracy % |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| none | 0.037, 0.006 | 95.0, 96.4 | 1.197, 0.744 | 57.8, 66.7 |
| batch norm | 0.100, 0.102 | 89.4, 90.6 | 2.356, 2.303 | 9.7, 10.8 |
| layer norm | 0.056, 0.122 | 92.2, 90.8 | 0.925, 0.826 | 66.7, 71.9 |
| RMSNorm | 0.041, 0.035 | 94.7, 95.6 | 1.578, 0.722 | 55.0, 72.8 |
| ScaleNorm | 0.102, 0.051 | 93.9, 94.7 | 1.409, 1.490 | 52.5, 38.3 |
| Dynamic Tanh | 1.850, 0.651 | 16.4, 61.9 | 1.850, 2.303 | 20.8, 8.6 |
| weight standardisation | 0.006, 0.041 | 95.0, 95.8 | 0.468, 0.635 | 80.0, 69.2 |
none: training loss (batch 64, mean of 2 seeds) 0.853 at step 50, 0.0214 at step 500; first below 0.1 at step 250.
batch norm: training loss (batch 64, mean of 2 seeds) 2.224 at step 50, 0.1014 at step 500; first below 0.1 at step never.
layer norm: training loss (batch 64, mean of 2 seeds) 0.800 at step 50, 0.0885 at step 500; first below 0.1 at step 250.
RMSNorm: training loss (batch 64, mean of 2 seeds) 0.796 at step 50, 0.0379 at step 500; first below 0.1 at step 250.
ScaleNorm: training loss (batch 64, mean of 2 seeds) 0.762 at step 50, 0.0762 at step 500; first below 0.1 at step 350.
Dynamic Tanh: training loss (batch 64, mean of 2 seeds) 2.252 at step 50, 1.2505 at step 500; first below 0.1 at step never.
weight standardisation: training loss (batch 64, mean of 2 seeds) 0.779 at step 50, 0.0232 at step 500; first below 0.1 at step 200.

Batch norm trained with batches of 4, test accuracy when each test batch of 4 is normalised with its own statistics: 10.6%, 12.5%.
RMS of the last hidden layer at initialisation (seed 1, 256 training images): none 5.49e-01, batch norm 7.25e-01, layer norm 7.19e-01, RMSNorm 8.13e-01, ScaleNorm 8.13e-01, Dynamic Tanh 7.41e-06, weight standardisation 1.96e-02.
\image(figures/normalization_digits.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Left: training-set cross-entropy (log scale) during training with batches of 64, mean of two seeds. Right: test accuracy of each seed for each normaliser, with batches of 64 (blue) and 4 (orange).)
\alttext(Left: the Dynamic Tanh line falls only from about 0.35 to about 0.1, a loss near 1.25; batch norm ends at about minus 1, layer norm and ScaleNorm near minus 1.1, RMSNorm near minus 1.4, and weight standardisation and no normalisation near minus 1.65. Right: blue points are between 89 and 97 percent for every normaliser except Dynamic Tanh at 16 and 62; orange points are between 38 and 80 percent except batch norm at 10 and 11 and Dynamic Tanh at 21 and 9.)

With batches of 64, the network fits the training set with or without normalisation. Within 500 steps, plain He initialisation and weight standardisation reach the lowest training loss (about 0.02, mean of two seeds), batch norm is the slowest of the working variants (0.10), and test accuracies of the working variants lie between 89.4% and 96.4% with no clear winner. A depth of 16 with careful initialisation is not deep enough for normalisation to be needed here.

Small batches separate the methods. With batches of 4, batch norm stops learning: the training loss stays at 2.36 and 2.30, at or above \(\ln10=2.30\), and test accuracy is 9.7% and 10.8%, chance level. This is not only a mismatch between batch and moving statistics. Normalising each test batch of 4 with its own statistics, as during training, gives only 10.6% and 12.5%. The per-example normalisers keep learning, at 38% to 73% after 500 noisy steps, and weight standardisation does best on average at 80.0% and 69.2%.
ScaleNorm is the least reliable of those (52.5% and 38.3%), and the spread between seeds shows that these short small-batch runs are far from converged.

Dynamic Tanh fails at both batch sizes: 16.4% and 61.9% with batches of 64, and 20.8% and 8.6% with batches of 4. The reason is visible before training. At initialisation the RMS of the last hidden layer is \(7.4\times10^{-6}\) with Dynamic Tanh, against 0.55 to 0.81 with no normalisation or any of the statistical normalisers, as the factor-\(\alpha\) argument predicts (\(0.5^{16}\approx1.5\times10^{-5}\)). Dynamic Tanh was designed for Transformers, whose residual connections carry the signal around each block. In a plain stack it shrinks the signal at every layer.
Weight standardisation also shrinks the signal at initialisation (RMS 0.020), yet it trains well. A plausible cause of the shrinkage, not tested here, is that centring each unit's weights removes the response to the mean of the non-negative ReLU inputs.

> Conclusion

The normalisers in the library are one operation, standardisation over a set of axes, and differ only in which axes they use. All of them match hand loops to rounding. Only batch norm puts the batch axis in the set, which makes it the only one that couples examples, and the only one that fails with tiny batches. The invariances are exact apart from \(\epsilon\). That matters once the input variance approaches \(\epsilon\), where the normaliser shrinks the input instead of rescaling it.

The Jacobian explains the effect on training. It annihilates the constant vector and, up to \(\epsilon\), the centred input, so the gradient into a normaliser is orthogonal to both. Weights feeding a normaliser are scale-invariant, so their gradients are orthogonal to them. Under SGD their norm therefore grows by exactly \(\eta^2\lVert g\rVert^2\) per step, lowering the effective learning rate.
Weight decay counteracts this. With enough gradient signal the norm settles at the predicted equilibrium. Once the training loss is near zero, the norm collapses and jumps back up periodically with spikes in the loss.

In depth, normalisation keeps activations near \(\sqrt{1/2}\) regardless of the weight gain, while unnormalised ReLU stacks follow \(c^l\). On a 16-layer digits MLP with good initialisation and batches of 64, this made no difference. With batches of 4 it separated batch norm from the per-example methods. The statistics-free Dynamic Tanh failed in this plain stack, because nothing restores the signal it shrinks.

